   THACH, RE},
Title = {CROSS-LINKING OF HEMIN TO A SPECIFIC SITE ON THE 90-KDA FERRITIN
   REPRESSOR PROTEIN},
Journal = {PROCEEDINGS OF THE NATIONAL ACADEMY OF SCIENCES OF THE UNITED STATES OF
   AMERICA},
Year = {1991},
Volume = {88},
Number = {14},
Pages = {6068-6071},
Month = {JUL},
Abstract = {Incubation of a 90-kDa ferritin repressor protein (FRP) with small
   amounts of radiolabeled hemin resulted in the formation of a strong
   interaction between the two that was stable to SDS/PAGE. (We refer to
   this interaction as a ``crosslink,{''} without intending to imply
   knowledge as to its chemical nature.) Of seven other proteins tested
   individually, only apohemopexin and bovine serum albumin showed similar
   crosslinking ability, albeit to a much lower extent. {[}C-14]Hemin
   specifically crosslinked to FRP in the presence of a 50-fold excess of
   total wheat germ proteins. Inclusion of catalase did not prevent the
   reaction of hemin with FRP, suggesting that H2O2 is not involved. The
   subsequent addition of a stoichiometric amount of apohemopexin did not
   reverse the reaction. Exhaustive digestion of the complex with
   Staphylococcus aureus V8 protease produced a major labeled peptide of 17
   kDa. These results show the existence of a highly specific, uniquely
   reactive hemin binding site on FRP.},
