Year = {2026},
Month = {2026 FEB 10},
Abstract = {This paper explores the social-ecological changes experienced by the
   Digo community of coastal Kenya, using an assemblage lens to present a
   composite narrative that captures the community's complex interactions
   with modernisation, environmental change, and cultural adaptation. This
   relational research approach conceptualises social, ecological, and
   material elements as dynamically connected and continuously reconfigured
   wholes, emphasising uncertainty, heterogeneity, and processes of
   becoming over fixed structures. Through narratives gathered in the FoRel
   project, which employed participatory Forum Theatre, we offer a grounded
   perspective on how these coastal communities navigate tensions between
   traditional values and global forces. The presented assemblage,
   including researchers as active participants, highlights the layered
   dynamics of coastal life, from resource conflicts to intergenerational
   shifts in digital literacy and identity. The narrative reveals that the
   obstacles to climate change adaptations often stem from Digo social
   life, bringing together disparate topics and exemplifying why coastal
   communities like the Digo may be disempowered in the face of such
   environmental change(s). This study contributes to less fragmented
   understandings of environmental and climate change by focusing on the
   entangled, relational dimensions of human-nature interactions through
   research participants' accounts of their daily practices and
   relations.Key policy highlightsClimate adaptation policies must account
   for the entangled social, cultural, and ecological realities of coastal
   communities. Interventions that overlook relational dynamics may
   reinforce disempowerment.Local narratives reveal that adaptation
   challenges are deeply embedded in everyday life. Participatory and
   arts-based methods can surface nuanced community perspectives often
   missed by top-down approaches.Efforts to improve coastal resilience must
   consider intergenerational tensions and digital transformations. These
   influence identity, values, and engagement with natural resource
   use.Policies must recognise informal and emotional infrastructures-like
   trust, religion, and kinship-as integral to how communities navigate
   change.Assemblage thinking offers a way to see beyond fragmented
   interventions, helping policymakers address multiple, interacting
   sources of vulnerability and potential},
