Title = {Increasing biodiversity and ecosystem functions along an age gradient of
   urban greenspaces},
Journal = {LANDSCAPE AND URBAN PLANNING},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {273},
Month = {SEP},
Abstract = {Urban greenspaces are constructed on soils disturbed by human
   activities, raising concerns about their capacity to sustain
   biodiversity and ecosystem functions. Uncertainty over whether these
   soils can recover toward conditions observed in natural ecosystems, as
   well as over the rates, mechanisms, and limits of soil development, has
   reinforced the perception that urban soils are irreversibly degraded.
   Here, we examined soil properties, microbial communities, and functional
   attributes across 30 urban greenspace sites spanning an age gradient in
   Seoul, Republic of Korea, using nearby natural ecosystems as references.
   Soil conditions improved with greenspace age, reflected in the
   accumulation of soil organic matter. These improvements were associated
   with higher microbial diversity and microbial gene copy numbers, along
   with increased potential metabolic capacity and shifts in functional
   attributes. Older sites harbored more plant-associated microbes and
   complex carbon decomposers, whereas taxa associated with pathogens and
   pollutants declined. Functional profiles showed increased
   ectomycorrhizal and saprotrophic functions, together with a broader
   potential for carbon metabolism in older sites. These findings identify
   soil organic matter as a key driver of ecological development in urban
   greenspaces and underscore the need to shift management from short-term,
   aesthetic-focused maintenance toward long-term strategies that enhance
   and maintain soil organic matter to support ecosystem services and
   resilience.},
